COMMAND NAME: mppstart.sh

Starts a Greenplum Database database management server instance.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mppstart.sh [-d <data_directory>] [-n | -z] [-m] [-y] [-s] [-a] [-q] 
[-l <logfile_directory>]

mppstart.sh [-t | -? | -v]

*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mppstart.sh script is analogous to functionality offered 
by the PostgreSQL pg_ctl and postmaster commands, which are used 
to start the database server. When you start a Greenplum Database database 
management server instance, you are actually starting several postmaster 
processes at once (the MPP master and all of the segment instances). 
The mppstart.sh script handles the startup of the individual instances. 
Each instance is started sequentially by default.

Before you can start a Greenplum Database database server, you must have 
initialized the system using mppcreatecluster.sh first.

*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-d <data_directory>

Optional. The master host data directory. If not specified, the 
value set for $MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.

-n

Optional. Starts the segment instances in parallel. In this mode, 
the startup process does not wait for return codes from the segment instances. 
The default behavior is to start the segment instances sequentially 
and wait for their return codes.

-z

Optional. Forces the startup of a segment instance with an invalid status. 
This may be desirable if the segment instance or host as a minor error that 
prevented startup in the first place. This option will allow you to start the 
segment instance (after fixing a problem) if it was marked as invalid in a 
previous startup attempt. The default behavior is to not start segment 
instances with an invalid status. If the segment instance is up and running 
then it will be marked valid again next time the MPP master tries to connect 
to it.

-m

Optional. Starts the MPP master instance only, which may be useful for 
maintenance tasks.

-y

Optional. Do not start the standby master host. The default is to start 
the standby master host and synchronization process.


-s

Rollover the log file for the MPP master instance. Moves the current 
logfile to <log file>.YYYYMMDD and starts a new log file.

-a

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.

-q

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, but is still 
written to the log file.

-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/mppAdminLogs.

-t

Displays the settings that will be used to start the system, such as the 
master data directory and port, and the name, data directory, port, valid 
status, and primary or mirror status of all the segment instances.

-?

Displays the online help.

-v

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and checksum of this script.



*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Start a Greenplum Database database management server instance:

mppstart.sh

Force start of the system if it has failed segment instances from a 
previous startup attempt:

mppstart.sh -z

Start all segment instances in parallel (no return code status):

mppstart.sh -n

Start the MPP master instance only:

mppstart.sh -m

Display the online help for the mppstart.sh script:

mppstart.sh -?


